**The claim is half right and its operative half is wrong.** Part (i) — "check 7 is stale" — I cannot refute; the plan itself mandates the relocation at `docs/cycle27-plan.md:2480-2483` (Pre-decided 53(d⁗)), which postdates the cycle-60 brief and rests on a measurement, not on a story. Part (ii) — "the script is therefore safe to run as written, and the right action is to proceed" — does not survive. Four points as requested.

---

## 1. The strongest reason the claim is wrong: `c60c_astcheck.py` is **unsatisfiable** for this brief, so its eleven PASSes are worth nothing

This is not "one stale check among eleven good ones". Gate 7 and **gate 9** have identical provenance — `c60c_astcheck.py:16-17` says gate 9 checks "the routes **the brief** FORBIDS", and that brief is cycle 60's, the same brief gate 7 serves (`c60b_astcheck.py:14`). Gate 9's forbidden list contains **`build_invoke`**.

Now put the two together against step 4's actual requirement — *an object living on `Diagram #639`*:

- gate 7 forbids `move_in` — relocating it there;
- gate 9 forbids `build_invoke` — the one verb in this fleet that **creates on a chosen diagram**: `tools/gscript.py:2159` `def build_invoke(target, cls, method_id, location, diagram_index=0)`, and `:2194` `build_property` likewise. Your own plan already knows this: `docs/cycle27-plan.md:1780` — *"`build_property` … and `build_invoke` … both already take a `diagram_index`"*.

**No correct script for this task can pass this gate.** A gate that no correct script can satisfy is not measuring scripts; it is measuring nothing. And the same staleness argument that excuses gate 7 voids gate 9's PASS — you cannot treat the FAIL as noise and the PASSes as assurance when they come out of the same 2026-09-21-obsolete contract.

What actually remains of the eleven is gates 2/3/4/4b/5/6/8 — the fleet's *standing* prohibitions, which any well-formed script here passes. **Not one gate in `c60c_astcheck.py` tests anything this brief cares about**: not `g.open_panel`'s absence (your own headline prohibition, `diag_c64_s3b_row1.py:37`, from `archive/peer/2026-09-21-c64-openpanel-cap.md`), not `g.ensure_loaded`'s, not "row 2 absent", not "a save at every measured-legal point" (47(e), `cycle27-plan.md:1805-1808`), not "`move_in` called at most once". Those live in the script's **docstring** (`:36-47`) — prose assertions, which is exactly what a static gate exists to replace.

So the correct conclusion from "the named gate is stale" is **"this script has not been statically gated"**, not "this script is safe to run as written". The claim's third ruled-out option forecloses the fix by mis-describing it: `c60c` *is itself* "the predecessor's gate with its checks KEPT, re-aimed at this cycle's diagnostic" (`c60c_astcheck.py:3-4`) — a `c60d` that keeps 1-6/8, re-aims 7 to **"`move_in` is called at most once"** (a gate shape this fleet has already written — `tools/bench/c57d4_astcheck.log:13` *"guard: move_in is called at most once"*) and re-aims 9 at *this* brief's forbidden routes is strictly **stronger**, not "the same file minus the failing check". Your ruled-out bullet only excludes a gate that *differs only by dropping* check 7.

## 2. A different explanation you have not considered — and it is externally sourced

The failure is neither a stale check nor a finding about the script. It is a finding about **53(d⁗)**, and gate 7 is the *live* objection to it.

`docs/cycle27-plan.md:2473` states as **"A LAW OF THIS FLEET"**: *"WHAT SCRIPTING CREATES LANDS ON THE TOP-LEVEL DIAGRAM AND MUST BE MOVED IN."* That is false about LabVIEW and false about your own toolkit:

- NI: **New VI Object takes an `owner refnum` input**, and *"if an object resides within a substructure such as a loop, a Case structure, a sequence structure… that substructure owns the object"* — you wire the substructure's reference into `owner refnum` and the object is **born there**. ([Tutorial: Adding an Object to a VI Using VI Scripting](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview/page/tutorial-adding-an-object-to-a-vi-using-vi-scripting.html), [Obtaining References to Objects in a Known Target VI](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-MY/bundle/labview/page/obtaining-references-to-objects-in-a-known-target-vi-using-vi-scripting.html))
- Your fleet: `build_invoke`/`build_property` already expose `diagram_index` (`gscript.py:2159`, `:2194`).

So the top-level landing is a property of **how `OpCreateLocalRead_v0` was wired**, not a law. Under this reading cycle 60's phrase "attempt 1's **pointless** relocation" is exactly right and still current: you relocate because the creator was told to create on the top level. Note what that costs — every `move_in` drags a junk `Invoke` (`cycle59_s3a_recipe.log:85,:249`; `diag_c62_s3b_movein.log:53`; `build_d1_v0_run7.log:352` purged 23; `run9.log:365` purged 31), and purging that junk is the entire subject of cycles 62-64.

**Second alternative, cheaper to act on:** the failure is a defect in the *dispatch brief* (it names a gate that contradicts its own step 4), and this is the **second consecutive cycle** it has appeared — `c62f_astcheck.log:11` at 11:03 and `c64e_astcheck.log:11` at 13:49, the same normalised first-failing gate line. `tools/cycle_runner.py:253` builds its repeated-failure signature from exactly that ("first failing GATE line of the run, normalised, uids dropped, lower-cased, 60 chars"), and `tools/bench/cycle_runner.log:97` **already carries** `gate:fail 7 move_in is neither imported nor called called=true im` for the prior cycle. Under CLAUDE.md's firefighter rule, "report verbatim and proceed" a third time converts the next cycle to fable/low. (Caveat, stated plainly: `cycle_runner.py:243` excludes bookkeeping tools from the signal and I could not confirm from a grep whether `c60c_astcheck` is on that list — but `cycle_runner.log:97` is evidence that it is not.)

## 3. What would falsify the claim

Falsify **part (i)** (and vindicate gate 7 as a live finding): show that `OpCreateLocalRead_v0.vi` can bind its creation to a destination diagram — i.e. that its second navigation pair (`Class Name 2` / `index 2`, set to `""` / `0` at `diag_c64_s3b_row1.py:474-482`) is the creator's **owner**, the same pair `move_in` uses as its destination at `build_d1_v0.py:324-326`. If setting `Class Name 2 = "Diagram"`, `index 2 = <live index of #639>` produces a Local already on `#639`, then step 4 is the pointless relocation by name, 53(d⁗) is refuted, and gate 7 was never stale.

Falsify **part (ii)** as I have argued it: exhibit any gate in `c60c_astcheck.py` that would distinguish a correct cycle-64 script from an incorrect one. I claim there is none.

I would change my mind about the whole review if `OpCreateLocalRead_v0`'s second pair turns out not to be an owner and no op in the fleet can create on a nested diagram — then `move_in` is the only route, 53(d⁗) stands as an empirical law *about our tools*, and the action reduces to "write c60d, then run".

